The adaptation in crates/model/src/chemistry/abbreviations/replacement.rs
specializes the algorithm to two finite coordinate differences, preserving
math.hypot rounding when placing fixed abbreviation templates. It uses safe
Rust and does not include or invoke the Python interpreter.

The adaptations in crates/io/src/chemistry/cdxml/marks.rs and
crates/io/src/chemistry/cdxml/arrows.rs preserve native chemical-symbol sizes
and arrow geometry acceptance thresholds. The arrow specialization also retains
subnormal scaling and infinity/NaN handling. Both use safe Rust without
including or invoking the Python interpreter.

The specialization in crates/io/src/chemistry/cdxml/graphics/shapes.rs
preserves chemical-symbol size thresholds using the same compensated norm in
safe Rust.

The adaptation in crates/io/src/chemistry/cdxml/scene/circles.rs follows the
compensated float summation in Python/bltinmodule.c, builtin_sum_impl, from the
same pinned CPython release. Together with the arrow norm specialization, it
preserves the original aromatic-circle ownership thresholds without invoking
Python.

The adaptation in crates/io/src/chemistry/cleanup/numeric.rs uses the same
compensated float summation from Python/bltinmodule.c, builtin_sum_impl, to
preserve selection cleanup orientation. Its norm calculations reuse the audited
arrow specialization; neither operation includes or invokes the Python
interpreter.

The separate adaptation in src/naming/openclatura/python_int_set.rs follows
CPython 3.12 Objects/setobject.c: integer-set probing, copying, difference,
and resizing policies. It retains the Python-2.0 terms in the accompanying
complete LICENSE (including the licence history). This adaptation records
only the 3.12 series: its exact patch release/commit is unresolved. The
3.12.12 / 4a5632fb pin above applies to the numeric adaptations, and is not
asserted for python_int_set.rs. The integer-set implementation is safe Rust
and neither includes nor invokes the Python interpreter.
